i don't know whether to be excited or  scared hey what's up guys my name is  zacchano  and welcome to vulcan week i'm gonna try  and keep this as short as possible  because it's supposed to start in like  25 minutes and i have to quickly  chuck this video up but basically what  vulcan week is is me  live streaming this entire week monday  to friday  10 a.m australian eastern standard time  to 6 p.m every single day this week  i will be live streaming on twitch.tv  slash the channel and i will be  implementing like or adding vulcan  support  to hazeldev's renderer so for those of  you who don't know hazel dev currently  and hazel itself as well the game engine  series  they both use opengl at the moment and  for numerous reasons i've wanted to  actually  add vulcan support for quite a while and  i thought that the best  the best way to deal with this is just  to probably just sit down and just work  at it all week don't get distracted with  anything else just sit down and just  work really hard and just do it all in  one week and that is what is happening  this week now i want to make a few notes  here first of all why are we adding  vulcan support  i mentioned recently that i wanted to go  back and polish a lot of what hazel  has to offer the previous halo devlog  which i'll link up there actually talks  a lot about you know how good is good  enough and do we want to go back and  kind of you know make  things better and i decided that yes i  did want to go back and make things  better this is part of that kind of  polishing pass i want it's really  important for me to get vulcan down  and like for hazel to start supporting  that for  quite a lot of reasons to be honest  primarily because it's going to lay  the groundwork the foundation for the  rest of  hazel's renderer opengl is not a very  uh how should we say this normal  rendering api there are a lot of  concepts in there that are missing or  that we have to emulate when we deal  with other apis  such as directx or even even dirt x11  not to mention directx 12  and vulcan as well and getting some of  those concepts down structuring our  renderer around  vulcan instead of around opengl is going  to  actually lay a lot of groundwork for us  to be able to later implement directx a  lot more easily or metal  if we choose to go down that route as  well but the other big difference is  that vulcan obviously supports  a lot more things than opengl does  namely ray tracing one of my goals  this year is to actually add ray tracing  to hazel i want to be able to do  something ray trace whether that be like  ray trace reflections or shadows or  you know global elimination something  like that i want to have some form of  ray tracing  in hazeldev by the end of the year and  obviously to make that happen i'm going  to need to use either vulcan or directx  12.  welcome being the much more kind of  flexible api it supports more platforms  i can run it on windows mac although  that's  we'll see what happens with that linux  and android as well which means it's a  lot more flexible to me as a solo  developer to use rather than directx 12  which of course is windows and xbox only  so anyway because of all of those  reasons i think it's really good to add  vulcan support to hazel because it's  just going to lay the foundation for  very very exciting things to come in the  future as well as force me to actually  write  like a better renderer because it's the  way that vulcan is structured for those  of you who have used it it kind of  forces you to do that because it's a lot  more low level and you have to think a  lot more about what the gpu is actually  doing and what you want it to do  and where and how and all that stuff now  let's talk about  my experience with falcon in the past i  have been  i've been using vulcan since probably  before it  officially came out back when i worked  at ea we added it to our mobile engine  which also supported windows  and uh that was uh you know a fun  experience i didn't play too big of a  role in that part but i was exposed to a  lot of falcon codes a lot of testing all  of that stuff so that was my first  experience with vulcan  back then it was very uh  very dodgy it um you know frequently we  tried to get it to run on android and if  it crashed it just rebooted the android  device there were a lot of driver issues  and that was my first kind of  you know being like whoa that's this  vulcan thing needs this [ __ ] thing  needs some work to make it good  and since then i mean i know it's  improved a lot i haven't used it  too much since then i think the only  thing i've really done  is about nine or ten months ago i  basically took  the game engine series version of hazel  which just has like a 2d renderer  and i added vulcan support to that that  was  10 months ago and whilst that did work  and i did learn a lot more about vulcan  during that period of time i haven't  touched vulcan code  in about nine or ten months and what  that means  at least for me is i've basically  forgotten everything  like i i mean i have you know i know  what render passes ready command buffers  you know all of those  kind of pipelines i remember what how  that stuff loosely works  but i it's not like opengl where i could  probably sit down and just  you know write an opengl program or a  renderer that works on paper because i  just know all of the api so well  vulcan is not like that at all and to be  honest it's a lot more of a burst so i  don't think remembering something like  that is something that a lot of people  are able to actually do  uh so it's gonna be fun i think it's  gonna be very challenging  this week i don't know how much i'm  gonna get done this week i'm gonna give  it my best shot  there is a lot of stuff that has to be  done in hazeldev first before we can  even start vulcan  so i think last live stream that i did  which was on friday i started  uh basically rewriting the whole shader  compiler and the whole shader system  inside hazel  to use spur v which is like a binary  shader format instead of  basically what i was doing which was  just using the opengl compiler and then  actually  uh using like a parser that i wrote to  go through all the uniforms and figure  out their types and you know  create uniform buffers basically well we  didn't actually use uniform buffers but  like makeshift  client-side uniform buffers and all of  that stuff it was quite an interesting  little system that i've basically  started rewriting so that it all uses  spur-v spurvi's reflection  um but we can also compile stuff  actually online like at runtime and  it's it's pretty cool but there's that  needs to be done before we can even  start vulcan  so today's livestream is going to be  probably focused  on getting spurvi to actually work with  opengl first and then we're going to  start on vulcan so there's  a few a few like housekeeping things  that need to be done before we can like  dive in and actually start working on  the vulcan renderer  hopefully that's not going to take more  than just today to figure out and then  the rest of the week can be devoted to  vulcan  there's also a lot of planning i think  that needs to be done in order to  get this stuff off the ground anyway the  point is it's  a lot of hard work but i'm really  looking forward to it we'll see how we  go  twitch or tv for slash the channel the  live stream starts in like 20 minutes  according to this if i can get this  video out in time  so uh definitely check out the schedule  as well twitter tv  slash the channel schedule because that  will uh  you know in your time zone that'll show  you what hours i'm actually  streaming in so if you're interested in  catching any of those streams then  you can check that out now as for like  the video recordings of streams the vods  they're going to be available to twitch  subscribers and to patrons as well as  usual so patreon.com you'll get the vods  indefinitely  because on twitch they actually expire  after 14 days so if you're interested in  or if you're not catching the streams or  you want to watch them later as well  then you can uh help support me and get  access to all those  vods as well it's a win-win anyway i'm  really excited for this to be honest and  a little bit scared  as i mentioned it's not just going to be  about me implementing vulkan it's  also going to be about me learning  vulcan again because i feel i feel like  i've learnt it at least three times in  my life and i keep forgetting it because  if you don't keep using vulcan i feel  like it's just so complicated that  you'll just slowly forget  everything and then you'll have to start  again so it's going to be a lot of fun  uh  lots of uh lots of tears to be had i  think  but um hope to see you guys there and i  will see you next time one more thing i  wanted to add as well was that obviously  because of this there's not gonna be any  videos this week i think  no there's going to be one on friday i  believe but apart from that like normal  videos are not really going to be  happening this week instead we're doing  this baltimore  thing so yeah anyway see you guys there  [Music]  goodbye  [Music]  you

